From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 19:40:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 153/184] The firmware visit was being halted mid-exchange by
 the screen pump

Why is even the working path slow? Every firmware read under the
timeshare fails at stage 3 -- no sector announced -- and D: only comes
up after ~6 such failures burn ~20 seconds and the fallback hands the
drive to our own reads. The same firmware call reads LBA 1600 on the
first try in the loader's wake, every boot. The cleanest difference
between those two environments turned out to be uncovered:

IOFW halts the sub CPU to copy the screen out of PRG-RAM. Bit 3 of
$FF8022 is the interlock the sub raises to hold it off, and cdc_grab
and the level-4 handler both use it -- but _segacd_bios_read never
did. So the whole of every visit ran with the servant freezing this
CPU every few frames, mid-exchange, against a drive whose conversation
has one turn per 1/75 s and whose link dies when a turn is missed. The
loader's wake has no servant running at all.

The visit now holds the interlock end to end, and IOFW's forced-grab
threshold while interlocked rises from 30 frames to 600: half a second
of patience was a wedge-guard sized for cdc_grab's millisecond holds,
and grabbing anyway half a second into a multi-second visit re-created
the exact hazard the interlock exists to prevent. The wedge-guard
remains, ten seconds out; a frozen status line during a visit is the
cost of the drive answering.

If this is the starvation, firmware reads start succeeding under the
timeshare, the six-failure preamble never happens, and the first open
of D: is a firmware read that works -- the same call, the same disc,
the same console that Doom is snappy on. If stage 3 persists with the
servant provably held off, the starvation is elsewhere and this
eliminated the best-cited candidate.
---
 bios/scdapi.h  |  2 +-
 bios/segacd2.S | 13 +++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index f723c1f..a673879 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  21
+#define SCD_VERSION  22
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd2.S b/bios/segacd2.S
index 5396776..2d8fec9 100644
--- a/bios/segacd2.S
+++ b/bios/segacd2.S
@@ -431,6 +431,18 @@ _segacd_bios_read:
         move.l  #0x7C900,sp             // scratch stack, outside the swap
         lea     0x7C000,a5
 
+        // The servant may not halt this CPU while Sega's firmware is
+        // driving the drive. Bit 3 of $FF8022 is the interlock IOFW
+        // already honours for cdc_grab's transfers; the visit never
+        // held it, so IOFW froze the sub for a screen copy every few
+        // frames of every visit -- mid-exchange, against a drive whose
+        // conversation has one turn per 1/75 s. The loader's wake has
+        // no servant running, and the same firmware call that reads
+        // LBA 1600 first-try there starves at stage 3 here. That is
+        // the cleanest environmental difference between the two, and
+        // it was simply never covered.
+        ori.w   #0x0008,0xFF8022
+
         move.l  a6,44(a5)               // EmuTOS's stack, in memory: the
                                         // watchdog has to be able to find
                                         // it, and a6 does not survive a
@@ -763,6 +775,7 @@ brd_done:
 
         move.l  _segacd_bios_vec4,0x70  // give the firmware its vector back
         bsr     bios_xchg               // EmuTOS's world back into place
+        andi.w  #0xFFF7,0xFF8022        // the servant may halt us again
         move.w  36(a5),d0
         move.b  d0,0xFF8033             // EmuTOS's mask, as we found it
                                         // -- the three the visit needs
